# Copy to `.dev.vars` (gitignored) for local `wrangler dev`. Literal values only. Never commit the
# filled-in copy.
#
# THIS FILE IS ALSO THE "Deploy to Cloudflare" PROMPT LIST: Cloudflare reads it and asks for each key
# on the setup form. So it is kept SHORT ON PURPOSE — only what a first deploy actually needs. Every
# key added here becomes one more blank box a newcomer has to understand.
#
# Everything else is optional and documented in CONFIG.md. Add it later with:
#     wrangler secret put <NAME>          # secrets
#     ...or edit `vars` in wrangler.jsonc # non-secret config
#
# NS_SERVER, NS_PORTAL_ISS and PORTAL_HANDOFF_URL are NOT here: they live in
# wrangler.jsonc `vars` and are prompted from there. A key in both places is prompted twice and then
# shadowed by the config value at runtime — silently ignoring whatever was typed.

# ── The one secret to set before your first deploy. ──
# Comma-separated `user@domain` accounts that pass every gate. The integration console — the page that
# reports how this deployment is actually configured — is gated to these accounts by DEFAULT, so with
# nobody named it admits NOBODY, including you. Set it now and you never have a console you cannot open.
PORTAL_SUPERADMINS=""

# ── Optional: the softphone app integration. ──
# One key turns all of it on: app status columns, the profile-page activation controls, sign-in details.
# Leave blank and every app feature is inert, with no error — the NetSapiens-only features are unaffected.
# Writes additionally need RINGOTEL_WRITE_DOMAINS, which is fail-closed when empty. See CONFIG.md.
RINGOTEL_API_KEY=""

# ── Optional: who sees which feature. ──
# Every feature is gated to a role by DEFAULT — you need nothing here to get those defaults. To re-level
# one, switch it off, or grant it to named accounts, set PORTAL_FEATURES (JSON). Full level vocabulary,
# the feature registry, and worked examples: CONFIG.md → "Features and gating".
